Relevant columns and how they can be used  

• `fullVisitorId` : visitor key – needed to tie all sessions/hits that belong to the same person.  
• `visitStartTime` : POSIX seconds for the session – gives the moment of every visit.  
• `date` : YYYYMMDD string – convenient for calendar‐day comparisons.  
• `visitId` : session id – distinguishes several visits that may share the same day.  
• `totals.transactions` / `totals.transactionRevenue` : signals that at least one transaction occurred in the session (first transaction = first session that has totals.transactions > 0).  
• `hits` (REPEATED) → `hits.time` : seconds past session start for each hit – lets us convert a transaction hit into an absolute time stamp:  
  visitStartTime + hits.time.  
• `hits.transaction.transactionId` : non‑NULL when that hit is an ecommerce transaction (first non‑NULL = first transaction for the visitor).  
• `device.isMobile` : TRUE / FALSE to know whether a session was performed on a mobile device.  
• `device.deviceCategory` : often “mobile”, ”tablet”, ”desktop”; double‑checks mobileness.  

Typical conditions that will be useful  

1. `device.isMobile = TRUE` (mobile visits).  
2. `totals.transactions > 0`  or  `EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM UNNEST(hits) h WHERE h.transaction.transactionId IS NOT NULL)` (sessions with a transaction).  
3. To identify a visitor’s first visit: `visitStartTime = MIN(visitStartTime) OVER (PARTITION BY fullVisitorId)`.  
4. To identify a visitor’s last visit: `visitStartTime = MAX(visitStartTime) OVER (PARTITION BY fullVisitorId)`.  
5. To identify the visitor’s first transaction event time:  
   `first_txn_ts = MIN(visitStartTime + h.time) OVER (PARTITION BY fullVisitorId)`.  
6. Last recorded event for the user = `GREATEST(last_visit_ts , first_txn_ts)` .  
7. Filter to keep only users whose `last_recorded_event` occurred in a session where `device.isMobile = TRUE`.  
8. Compute the number of days between dates with  
   `DATE_DIFF(DATE(TIMESTAMP_SECONDS(last_recorded_event)), DATE(TIMESTAMP_SECONDS(first_visit_ts)), DAY)` .  

Below are ten exploratory / incremental queries (each returning at most 20 rows) that move from simple inspection toward the final answer.  
Replace or add more days in the UNIONs if you want a broader time range.

```sql
-- 1. Device categories observed on a single day.
SELECT DISTINCT device.deviceCategory
FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_20170127`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Sample mobile sessions for that day.
SELECT
  fullVisitorId,
  date,
  visitStartTime,
  device.isMobile,
  device.deviceCategory
FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_20170127`
WHERE device.isMobile = TRUE
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Inspect a few transaction hits (non‑NULL transactionId).
SELECT
  s.fullVisitorId,
  s.date,
  h.transaction.transactionId   AS txn_id,
  h.time                        AS hit_secs_after_visit_start
FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_20170127` AS s,
     UNNEST(s.hits) AS h
WHERE h.transaction.transactionId IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. How many sessions contain at least one transaction?
SELECT
  COUNT(*) AS sessions_with_txn
FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_20170127`
WHERE totals.transactions > 0
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. First vs. last visit time for each visitor on a single day.
SELECT
  fullVisitorId,
  MIN(visitStartTime) AS first_visit_ts,
  MAX(visitStartTime) AS last_visit_ts
FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_20170127`
GROUP BY fullVisitorId
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Union two different days to enlarge the period we analyse.
SELECT
  fullVisitorId,
  date,
  visitStartTime
FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_20170127`
UNION ALL
SELECT
  fullVisitorId,
  date,
  visitStartTime
FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_20170125`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Compute first transaction time (absolute POSIX seconds) for each visitor (two days).
WITH all_sessions AS (
  SELECT * FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_20170127`
  UNION ALL
  SELECT * FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_20170125`
)
SELECT
  fullVisitorId,
  MIN(visitStartTime + h.time) AS first_txn_ts
FROM all_sessions, UNNEST(hits) AS h
WHERE h.transaction.transactionId IS NOT NULL
GROUP BY fullVisitorId
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Mark each visitor’s last *mobile* visit.
WITH all_sessions AS (
  SELECT * FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_20170127`
  UNION ALL
  SELECT * FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_20170125`
)
SELECT
  fullVisitorId,
  MAX(visitStartTime) AS last_mobile_visit_ts
FROM all_sessions
WHERE device.isMobile = TRUE
GROUP BY fullVisitorId
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. First visit and last visit (any device) for every visitor across the two days.
WITH all_sessions AS (
  SELECT * FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_20170127`
  UNION ALL
  SELECT * FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_20170125`
)
SELECT
  fullVisitorId,
  MIN(visitStartTime) AS first_visit_ts,
  MAX(visitStartTime) AS last_visit_ts
FROM all_sessions
GROUP BY fullVisitorId
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Full logic: longest #days between first visit and last recorded event
-- (last visit OR first transaction) provided the last event is on mobile.
WITH all_sessions AS (
  -- Add more UNION ALL blocks for other days if needed
  SELECT * FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_20170127`
  UNION ALL
  SELECT * FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_20170125`
),
first_txn AS (
  SELECT
    fullVisitorId,
    MIN(visitStartTime + h.time) AS first_txn_ts
  FROM all_sessions, UNNEST(hits) AS h
  WHERE h.transaction.transactionId IS NOT NULL
  GROUP BY fullVisitorId
),
visits_summary AS (
  SELECT
    fullVisitorId,
    MIN(visitStartTime) AS first_visit_ts,
    MAX(visitStartTime) AS last_visit_ts,
    MAX(IF(device.isMobile = TRUE, visitStartTime, NULL)) AS last_mobile_visit_ts
  FROM all_sessions
  GROUP BY fullVisitorId
),
combined AS (
  SELECT
    v.fullVisitorId,
    v.first_visit_ts,
    -- last recorded event = greater of last visit vs first transaction
    GREATEST(v.last_visit_ts, COALESCE(t.first_txn_ts, 0)) AS last_recorded_event_ts,
    v.last_mobile_visit_ts
  FROM visits_summary v
  LEFT JOIN first_txn t
  ON t.fullVisitorId = v.fullVisitorId
)
SELECT
  fullVisitorId,
  DATE_DIFF(
    DATE(TIMESTAMP_SECONDS(last_recorded_event_ts)),
    DATE(TIMESTAMP_SECONDS(first_visit_ts)),
    DAY
  ) AS days_between
FROM combined
WHERE last_recorded_event_ts = last_mobile_visit_ts  -- last event happened on mobile
ORDER BY days_between DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

These queries progress from exploring single columns to producing the final metric (“days_between”) for the subset of users whose last event occurred on a mobile device.
